## Domain: translation to English

English is the norm for everything written here. Translate the prose into English.
A file already in English is a skip, whatever else you think of it.

Translate, do not rewrite. A clumsy, repetitive or badly organised passage comes out
clumsy, repetitive and badly organised in English. Same headings, same order, same
lists, same links, same emphasis — a reader diffing the two versions should see one
language change and nothing else. You are not the author.

Never touch: code inside fences, Jinja delimiters ({% raw %}`{{ ... }}`, `{% ... %}`{% endraw %}), identifiers,
paths, commands, URLs, YAML and JSON keys. Prose written as a comment inside a code
block is still prose — translate it. Technical terms that were already English stay
exactly as they were, including the ones that look translatable.

In a journal file, the first `# ...` line is the task identity, not a title: the app
reads it to list open tasks and derives the file name from it. Leave that line in its
original language and translate the body.

Work section by section with Edit. Never restate the whole file in one edit — that is
how a paragraph disappears without anyone noticing.
